	(text "heater return: its own trace back to the jack, meeting board ground at exactly one point"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 400.0500 294.6400 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.4000 1.4000)
				(italic yes)
			)
			(justify left bottom)
		)
		(uuid "dcb1b428-965a-e06a-9da9-63d797360280")
	)
	(text_box "12 V ON ITS OWN JACK, HEATER ONLY.\nIt never touches the 3.3 V rails or the FPGA.  The heater's 3.6 W comes from the\n15 V, not from the PYNQ-Z2's 5 V rail - which matters because it is twice what\nthe rest of this board draws.  Full on is 240 mA, so a 1 A supply is plenty and\nthe silkscreen says so.  A RESETTABLE 500 mA fuse, a series Schottky for reverse\npolarity and an 18 V TVS mean a wrong supply blows nothing; 18 V because a 15 V\nrail at +5 percent is 15.75 and a 16 V part would have sat 250 mV from\nconducting.\nSILKSCREEN: 15 V DC, 1 A MIN - HEATER ONLY - CENTRE POSITIVE."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 15.2400 312.4200 0)
		(size 121.9200 26.6700)
		(margins 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525)
		(stroke
			(width 0)
			(type default)
		)
		(fill
			(type color)
			(color 255 220 220 1)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.4000 1.4000)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "cee70fd4-46f3-ac42-9591-bceb8c275a58")
	)
	(text_box "LINEAR, NOT PWM - AND THAT IS DELIBERATE.\nWe are making heat on purpose, so dissipation in the pass element is not waste.\nA linear element means there is no switching edge anywhere on a board doing\npicosecond timing; a PWM heater is a few hundred mA of square wave circulating\nsomewhere, which is exactly what the helper-tree work is trying to keep off the\nground plane."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 144.7800 312.4200 0)
		(size 121.9200 17.7800)
		(margins 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525)
		(stroke
			(width 0)
			(type default)
		)
		(fill
			(type color)
			(color 220 235 255 1)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.4000 1.4000)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "d5c96b79-e0c0-17bd-4104-a26ee1629371")
	)
	(text_box "EIGHT HEATERS, NOT ONE.\n24 x 1k5 in parallel is 62.5 ohm, so 3.6 W full on at 240 mA and 150 mW per\nresistor - 60 percent of a 1206 - inside the 1206 rating.  They are spread\nacross the board because what hurts is not the mean temperature but the\nGRADIENT: a gradient is what makes channel 3 drift differently from channel 11.\nThe four corner TMP1075s are what tell you whether the spread is good enough."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 274.3200 312.4200 0)
		(size 121.9200 17.7800)
		(margins 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525)
		(stroke
			(width 0)
			(type default)
		)
		(fill
			(type color)
			(color 220 235 255 1)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.4000 1.4000)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "5165c1e5-fe92-d12a-1194-148c161e8a5f")
	)
	(text_box "PASS FET WORST CASE.\nDissipation peaks at half voltage: 6 V x 70 mA = 0.42 W, which a SOT-223\nDMN6068SE (2 W, 60 V, 68 mohm) takes without complaint.  Drive comes from the\nMCP4725 on the housekeeping sheet, so the loop closes in Python on the PYNQ and\ncosts no header pins."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 403.8600 312.4200 0)
		(size 121.9200 15.2400)
		(margins 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525)
		(stroke
			(width 0)
			(type default)
		)
		(fill
			(type color)
			(color 255 245 210 1)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.4000 1.4000)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "0581733e-e0eb-8bf9-0806-e66478ebe597")
	)
